      "math_academy_id": "e-376",
      "problem": "What is the antiderivative of $\\dfrac {3+2x} {x} ?$",
      "worked_solution": "We separate the integrand into the sum of two fractions so that we can simplify each term. So, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{3+2x}{x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int (\\frac{3}{x}+\\frac{2x}{x})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int (\\frac{3}{x}+2)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =3\\int \\frac{1}{x}\\,\\text{d}x+2\\int \\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =3\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x|+2x+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLastly, let's rewrite the constant so that we can combine it with the natural logarithm. For any constant $C,$ we can always write $C =3 \\ln K$ where $K$ is some other constant. Doing this allows us to combine the constant with $3\\ln|x|$ term, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{3+2x}{x}\\,\\text{d}x & =3\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x|+2x+C \\\\  & =3\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x|+2x+3\\operatorname{ln}⁡K \\\\  & =3\\operatorname{ln}⁡(K|x|)+2x.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
